Epiglottitis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Drooling/dysphagia, Tripod posturing, Rapid onset of toxic appearance

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-020
Reason for Visit: Abrupt, rapid onset of severe sore throat, high fever, and drooling requiring immediate, critical assessment of life-threatening airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a child sitting upright, refusing to lie down, drooling continuously, and looking profoundly unwell with a high fever. Immediate, emergent assessment was mandated to evaluate imminent complete airway obstruction and coordinate rapid, safe transfer to a definitive care facility prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They suddenly got a very high fever, they can't swallow their own spit, and they look absolutely terrified to breathe."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an abrupt, rapid, and severe onset of high fever and marked systemic toxicity developing over just 4 hours. There is a severe sore throat and localized dysphagia explicitly resulting in continuous, unrelenting drooling. The voice is muffled, thick, and has a 'hot potato' quality, definitively caused by severe supraglottic localized edema. There is an absolute absence of a barking cough. The child exhibits severe anxiety and extreme restlessness. The parents note the child missed several early childhood immunizations, including Hib.
Functional Status: Acutely toxic, sitting completely still to maintain airway patency.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of complete respiratory arrest.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/50 | HR: 160 | Temp: 39.5C | RR: 45 | SpO2: 91% | Wt: 16 Kg
General: Profoundly toxic, ill clinical appearance. Extreme anxiety.
Physical Exam: The patient is explicitly demonstrating tripod posturing characterized by sitting upright, leaning forward, and extending the neck to mechanically open the airway. Continuous drooling of saliva is observed. Late inspiratory stridor is present, indicating imminent, life-threatening complete airway obstruction. No attempt was made to visualize the oropharynx with a tongue depressor to strictly avoid precipitating fatal laryngospasm. The diagnostic tetrad of drooling, tripod posturing, and rapid toxic onset is vividly confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Parents are highly distressed.
Medication Reconciliation: Refused all oral medications due to severe dysphagia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Epiglottitis - Status: Critical Emergency (Imminent airway obstruction)
Differentials:

Viral Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis) - Ruled out explicitly by the extremely rapid onset, profound toxic appearance, severe drooling, and the absolute absence of a barking cough.

Retropharyngeal Abscess - Ruled out clinically by the speed of onset (hours rather than days) and the prominent tripod posturing, though definitive exclusion requires imaging in a hospital setting.

Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out by the presence of a high fever, systemic toxicity, and the specific muffled 'hot potato' voice indicative of supraglottic edema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical (Immediate threat to life)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of drooling, high fever, and tripod posturing constituted an absolute, critical medical emergency requiring immediate, on-site after-hours triage. Telephone triage identified the hallmarks of imminent upper airway occlusion. Objective evaluation immediately confirmed acute epiglottitis based on the classic diagnostic tetrad, explicitly distinguishing it from benign viral croup. Attempting to delay assessment, or transporting the child via private vehicle, carried a near-certain risk of complete airway collapse and death. Immediate clinical intervention involved securing the environment, explicitly prohibiting any examination of the throat, keeping the child perfectly calm, and activating emergency medical services (000) for a highly controlled, advanced airway transport.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Activated 000 for immediate priority ambulance dispatch with advanced airway capabilities. Administered highly concentrated blow-by oxygen via parent without touching the child's face to avoid agitation. Absolutely prohibited any oral examination or supine positioning.
Positive Behavioral Change: The parents must actively project absolute, unnatural calmness. Do not physically restrain, force to lie down, or forcibly inspect the mouth of the child. Providing a calm, completely non-invasive environment directly prevents catastrophic anxiety-induced laryngospasm, physically preserving the fragile remaining airway until intubation is possible.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to intensive care retrieval team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate handover to Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance). Do not transport by private car under any circumstances.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a life-threatening critical emergency requiring immediate hospital-based definitive airway management.
Clinical Course: The child requires intubation in an operating theatre setting and intravenous antibiotics; survival is dependent on preventing airway closure before intubation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Wait precisely in the current position for the ambulance. Do not attempt to look in the child's mouth, and do not lay the child flat.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The flap of tissue covering the windpipe is severely infected and swollen, creating an imminent risk of suffocating the child. The most dangerous thing to do right now is to upset the child or force them to lie flat, as this will slam the airway entirely shut.
Actionable Steps: Stay completely still. Speak in whispers. Allow the child to sit in exactly the posture they have chosen.

